## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical online data-structure thinking for a sliding-window distinct problem, common in real-time analytics pipelines used by data platforms at Snowflake, Salesforce, and Instacart.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance
- Hash map with counts
- Eviction policy and amortized analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo
let window = []; // deque of last W ids
let counts = new Map();
function add(id){
  window.push(id);
  counts.set(id, (counts.get(id) || 0) + 1);
  if (window.length > W){
    const old = window.shift();
    const c = counts.get(old) - 1;
    if (c === 0) counts.delete(old); else counts.set(old,c);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for approximate distinct in extremely high-throughput streams?
- What happens if events arrive in irregular batch sizes or with possible duplicates within a batch?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming cardinality with bounded memory using probabilistic data structures; beginner-friendly but shows real trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for distinct count estimation
- Hashing, leading-zero counting, registers
- Time: O(1) per item, Memory: O(m)
- Error ~1.04/√m; choose m from m ≈ (1.04/ε)^2

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal HyperLogLog sketch (illustrative)
class HLL {
  constructor(p = 12) {
    this.m = 1 << p;
    this.reg = new Uint8Array(this.m);
  }
  add(item) {
    const h = hash64(item); // 64-bit int hash
    const p = Math.log2(this.m);
    const j = Number(h >>> (64 - p));
    const w = h & ((1n << BigInt(64 - p)) - 1n);
    const rank = 1 + clz64(w);
    if (this.reg[j] < rank) this.reg[j] = rank;
  }
  estimate() {
    // bias-corrected estimator placeholder
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you pick p for a given error? Explain m↔ε relation.
- What tests would you run to validate accuracy on synthetic streams?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests basic algorithmic analysis and trade-offs between brute force and divide-and-conquer techniques.

## Key Concepts
- Time complexity and space usage
- Inversions problem and two standard approaches
- Trade-offs with input size and data order

## Code Example
```javascript
function countInversionsNaive(a){
  let c=0;
  for(let i=0;i<a.length;i++){
    for(let j=i+1;j<a.length;j++){
      if(a[i]>a[j]) c++;
    }
  }
  return c;
}
function countInversionsMergeSort(a){
  function sortCount(arr){
    if(arr.length<=1) return {array: arr, inv:0};
    const mid = Math.floor(arr.length/2);
    const left = sortCount(arr.slice(0,mid));
    const right = sortCount(arr.slice(mid));
    let i=0,j=0, merged=[], inv= left.inv + right.inv;
    while(i<left.array.length && j<right.array.length){
      if(left.array[i] <= right.array[j]) merged.push(left.array[i++]);
      else { merged.push(right.array[j++]); inv += left.array.length - i; }
    }
    return {array: merged.concat(left.array.slice(i)).concat(right.array.slice(j)), inv: inv};
  }
  return sortCount(a).inv;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend the merge-count to return the actual inversion pairs?
- What changes if the input is already sorted or nearly sorted?
